# Route Conventions, Input/Output Schemas, Pagination & Idempotency

> Topic: Resource route conventions, strict input/output DTO separation (`CT2`), single error envelope (`CT3`), status codes (`CT4`), bounded pagination (`CT6`), and atomic idempotency (`CT10`) for {{project_name}}.

<!-- GEN: Why: Consolidate route creation, DTO separation, error envelope, status codes, pagination, and idempotency rules into a single cohesive reference file ({{ref_01}}) with project-concrete code examples. -->

## 1. Resource Naming, Path Hierarchy & HTTP Methods (`CT4`, `CT9`)

- **Plural nouns, kebab-case paths**: `/api/v1/work-orders`, `/api/v1/work-orders/{work_order_id}/items`. Never use verbs in resource paths (`/api/v1/createWorkOrder` is forbidden) unless modeling an explicit state-transition sub-resource (`POST /api/v1/work-orders/{work_order_id}/cancel`).
- **Nesting depth**: At most **2 levels** (`/parent/{parent_id}/children`). Deeper relationships are addressed by their own top-level resource with a filter query parameter (`GET /api/v1/tasks?work_order_id=...`).
- **Deterministic `operationId` and tags (`CT9`)**: Every endpoint declares an explicit `tags=[...]` and stable `<resource>_<action>` `operationId` so generated SDKs and contract diffs never churn on function renames.
- **Wire field casing**: Follow the project's canonical wire convention (`{{wire_field_casing}}`) consistently across path parameters, query parameters, request bodies, and response payloads.

